# Parser Subsystem Structure

The `org.jsoup.parser` package implements HTML and XML parsing through a layered architecture consisting of a tokeniser, a tree builder, and a state machine (source-org.jsoup.parser.package-info.java:1-3).

## Tokeniser

The `Tokeniser` converts raw input into a stream of tokens. It is constructed with a reference to a `TreeBuilder`, and it determines its output syntax (HTML vs XML) based on the concrete type of that tree builder:

```java
Tokeniser(TreeBuilder treeBuilder) {
    syntax = treeBuilder instanceof XmlTreeBuilder ? Document.OutputSettings.Syntax.xml : Document.OutputSettings.Syntax.html;
    ...
    this.reader = treeBuilder.reader;
    this.errors = treeBuilder.parser.getErrors();
}
```
(src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/Tokeniser.java:5-11)

The tokeniser maintains pending token objects (start tag, end tag, XML declaration) that get reused/updated as tokens are emitted, and it reads from the same `CharacterReader` that the tree builder owns (src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/Tokeniser.java:5-11). Its internal state can be transitioned by the tree builder — for example, when entering elements with special text-parsing rules (script, style, textarea, plaintext, etc.), the tree builder calls `tokeniser.transition(...)` to switch tokenising modes:

```java
switch (contextName) {
    case "script":
        tokeniser.transition(TokeniserState.ScriptData);
        break;
    case "plaintext":
        tokeniser.transition(TokeniserState.PLAINTEXT);
        break;
    case "template":
        tokeniser.transition(TokeniserState.Data);
        pushTemplateMode(HtmlTreeBuilderState.InTemplate);
```
(src/main/java/org/jsoup/parser/HtmlTreeBuilder.java:1-10, "initialise the tokeniser state" chunk)

and more generally:
```java
